VP9 decodes in hardware: VideoToolbox's VP9 decoder has to be registered first

video_toolbox.mm's header records that VP9 comes back -12906
(kVTCouldNotFindVideoDecoderErr) "even when fed a real config record" and
concludes the host has no VP9 decoder, which is why video_vp9_decode.mm is
software-only libvpx. The decoder is a supplemental one: VideoToolbox offers it
only after VTRegisterSupplementalVideoDecoderIfAvailable(kCMVideoCodecType_VP9).
Measured standalone on an Apple M4, macOS 27.2, with a vpcC record:
VTIsHardwareDecodeSupported = false and -12906 before the call; true, noErr and
a hardware session after it. (AV1 answers the same way after its own call; not
wired here.)

macapk_vtb_open_vp9 registers the decoder once and opens a session from the
stream's own parameters. video_vp9_decode.mm reads them from each keyframe's
uncompressed header (Android gives a VP9 codec no csd), opens or reopens the
session when they change, and forwards decode / poll / flush / close to it.
libvpx stays as the fallback: no hardware session, or MACAPK_VP9_HW=0. Both
engines hand out 32BGRA CVPixelBuffers, so nothing downstream changes.

Measured on Instagram 455, Reels (960-wide VP9 profile 0): "hw=YES
(VideoToolbox)" on every stream, 0 decode errors, picture correct; the thread
that ran libvpx (ExoPlayer:Playback) went from 8.1% to 1.1% of a core. The
process total did not move outside its noise (43-68% -> 42-54%): the Android main
thread, repainting the view tree in Java2D for every video frame, is 26-41% of a
core and is the cost that remains.

